// CORPORATE KOMBAT — VICE PRESIDENT — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// CORPORATE KOMBAT as an 80s action-movie / cop-show score — a game alternate of office,
// selectable from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// The office cabinet is the villain's corporate HQ; this is the hero storming the building, scored
// the way Faltermeyer and Jan Hammer scored 1984–86 (Axel F, Top Gun, Miami Vice, the Beverly Hills
// Cop chase cues). None of those records is quoted: the music is CORP FUNKHY 4's.
// 
// THE TUNE is Peter's MELODY A (office.js lead25, as played), note for note with its own lengths —
// the overlaps are its slides — with its four endings, the B melody, the wind-down and the ending.
// Verse at the hook's pitch, chorus an octave up, the climax a whole step higher (A minor).
// 
// THE HERO LEAD: a song-local Classic Mono turned Faltermeyer (square + detuned saw + sub square,
// one 24 dB filter with a quick pluck, legato, 70 ms glide), Peter's exciter on the lane, a plain
// triple square (initSquare) an octave under it in the choruses. A 16th SEQUENCER BASS (a second
// Classic Mono, plucked short) on the office's walking roots over a sine sub; synth-brass fanfares
// and answers on the Jupiter brass (bestPwmBrass, the one PWM voice); the sampler orchestra hit
// (mrdrOrchHit); the 80s gated snare (snare80sGated → room → gate) stacked with the Eighties snare
// and claps; Simmons toms (sdsTom*) gated on Group 1; timpani on G, D and A (timpaniHit retuned);
// the Eighties kick, hats and crash; DX7 bell piano comp (mrdrDx7Keys); string machine
// (synthStrings); a 16th square arpeggio (squareTone2). No CRLS-1 voice.
// 
//   1–4    LOBBY — G-major muzak through the lobby speaker; the sequencer pulses a G pedal outside it; timpani and gated toms kick the door in
//   5–8    BREACH — G minor, 128: orchestra hit, the 16th bass, kick on the quarters, the brass fanfare in the hook's rhythm
//   9–16   THE HOOK — MELODY A on the hero lead (its own pitch), gated snare, DX comp; brass answers from 13
//   17–24  CHORUS — the hook an octave up, the square an octave under it, brass answers, strings, orchestra hits, stacked snare and claps
//   25–28  B — the B melody over brass swells, orchestra hits on E♭ and D, the riser, D7 brass hits
//   29–36  FLOOR 23 — the hook again over a 16th square arpeggio; the square and the comp back at 33; a tom run into the chase
//   37–40  THE CHASE — the sequencer alone with G and D timpani and gated toms in 3-3-2, a ticking sidestick, orchestra stabs
//   41–44  THE CHASE, TWO — the B melody over the chase toms, the kit back in, the riser and a snare roll into the lift
//   45–52  CLIMAX — the key lift: a whole step up into A minor, everything in; bar 52 turns Dm → D7 for the loop
//   53–56  AFTERMATH — E♭add9 Cm7 Am7♭5 D7, half time, the lead's long notes, a timpani roll
//   57–60  END CREDITS — the hook once more, the D7 hits, G MAJOR
// 
// Harmony: the office's, a beat at a time in the hook (Gm7 · Gm7/B♭ · Cm7 · Gm7/D | the same |
// B♭6 | Cm7 D), B as E♭add9 → E♭maj7 | D | Cm11 Gm/C Cm7 | D7; the climax the same a tone up
// (Am7 … | C6 | Dm7 E), its last bar Dm7 → D7 so the loop lands on Gm7; the lobby G6 C6 D6 Em7
// Cadd9 Am7; the end on G major.
// 
// Levels by measurement (solo LUFS through the master chain, against the lead): in the chorus
// the square 8vb −6, brass answers −3.5, strings −8, comp −10.5, bass −1, kick 0, the snare
// stack −3, hats −13 to −15; the brass swells and the riser about −6.5; in the chase the
// timpani 5 and the gated toms 7–8 under the sequencer bass; in the lobby the vibes loudest.
// Trailing master gain -6.3 dB: the whole song reads −20.9 LUFS, peak −3.9 dBFS.
// 60 bars at 128, 113 s; the loop 5–52 is 90 s.
